 ## How criterion 3 is read for a tool that evaluates a kernel
 
 8 November 2026: some tools evaluate an SPK and do not hand back its stored records (ANISE's Python API). For those, the file is carried when every epoch is the written one and no state at a node epoch is further from the written state than SPICE's own evaluation of the same kernel is. Rule set by the apps session, confirmed by the seat on 8 November. Nothing was pre-registered for evaluating tools.
+
+8-9 November 2026 amendment, before any real GMAT SPK comparison was seen: for a tool whose only readout is a fixed-precision text report, its states are compared with SPICE's evaluation of the same kernel printed at the same precision, so both pass through the same readout. The bar is unchanged. The reason: GMAT's report prints at most 16 significant digits, and a stand-in that returns SPICE's own states, equal to SPICE in all 25,926 values at 16 digits, fails the unamended rule (2.18e-11 km against SPICE's 1.46e-11 km), so the rule as first written could not be met by any tool read through that report. It applies to GMAT only today; Orekit, Basilisk and ANISE keep the full-double comparison. Amendment by the seat, written by the apps session.
diff --git a/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/RESULTS.md b/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/RESULTS.md
index 107c0ede9..baec00947 100644
--- a/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/RESULTS.md
+++ b/docs/design/evidence/c7-flight-dynamics-adapters/RESULTS.md
@@ -87,3 +87,13 @@ elapsed time in A.1 seconds; the kernel's nodes are TDB, and over 30 days the TD
 moves them about 0.28 ms apart. The script now stops each step at the next node's TDB epoch
 (`TDBModJulian` stop condition) instead of stepping by elapsed time. The comparison and its bar are
 unchanged.
+
+**The comparison through GMAT's report.** GMAT's states reach the notebook only through a text report
+of at most 16 significant digits, and a double needs 17. A stand-in that returns SPICE's own states,
+equal to SPICE in every value at 16 digits, fails the evaluating-tool rule as first written
+(2.18e-11 km against SPICE's 1.46e-11 km). By the 8-9 November amendment in NAMING.md, written before
+any real GMAT SPK comparison was seen, GMAT's states are compared with SPICE's evaluation printed at
+the same 16 digits; the bar is unchanged, and Orekit, Basilisk and ANISE keep the full-double
+comparison. Future work: read GMAT's states at full precision instead, for example from an ephemeris
+file GMAT writes (an SPK read back by SPICE at the nodes), which would need no amendment but puts
+GMAT's own writer in the path.
